```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported fault — Office A host 10.1.0.100 being unable to reach the Office B
server at 10.5.0.4 — does not exist in the live lab. Repeated ICMP probes from
pc2 (10.1.0.101, the same /24 as the reporter's device) to srv1 (10.5.0.4) show
6/6 packets delivered with sub-2 ms round-trip time and zero loss. TCP connections
to srv1 reach the host (ports return "Connection refused", not timeout), confirming
the data path is fully intact in both directions. OSPF has converged on all
distribution and core switches (dswa1, dswa2, dswb1, dswb2, csw1, csw2) with
correct ECMP paths across both inter-building uplinks (CD_CORE_1_DSWB1/B2 and
CD_CORE_2_DSWB1/B2). The inter-building link the user claims is "always down" is
demonstrably up.

The only anomaly found is a standing iptables/nftables ACL on both dswa1 and dswa2
that permits ICMP but drops all other traffic from 10.1.0.0/24 toward 10.3.0.0/24
(pc3's subnet). This ACL is embedded in the baseline startup scripts of both devices
with the comment "mirrors Cisco named ACL" — it is deliberate security policy, not
a newly injected change. It does not touch 10.5.0.0/24 at all, and its counter hits
(4 ICMP accepted, 6 TCP dropped during this investigation) are entirely from our own
diagnostic probes, not user traffic.

Root cause: The helpdesk ticket is a false premise. No fault was introduced; the
Office A → Office B path to 10.5.0.4 has been working throughout. No corrective
action is required for the reported symptom.
=== END ===
```